## Why This Is Asked

This tests ability to implement a small reusable cross‑platform UI component with proper touch feedback and accessibility.

## Key Concepts

- Cross‑platform input feedback with Pressable
- Accessibility props
- Reusable component design
- Flexbox layout and styling

## Code Example

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { View, Text, Pressable, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';
import Icon from 'react-native-vector-icons/MaterialIcons';

export const SettingsRow = ({ icon, label, onPress, disabled }) => (
  <Pressable
    onPress={onPress}
    disabled={disabled}
    android_ripple={{ color: 'rgba(0,0,0,.15)' }}
    style={({ pressed }) => [
      styles.row,
      pressed && !disabled && styles.pressed,
      disabled && styles.disabled,
    ]}
    accessibilityRole="button"
    accessibilityState={{ disabled }}
  >
    <Icon name={icon} size={24} style={styles.icon} />
    <Text style={styles.label}>{label}</Text>
    <Icon name="chevron-right" size={24} style={styles.chevron} />
  </Pressable>
);

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  row: { flexDirection: 'row', alignItems: 'center', padding: 12 },
  icon: { marginRight: 12 },
  label: { flex: 1, fontSize: 16 },
  chevron: { color: '#888' },
  pressed: { opacity: 0.75 },
  disabled: { opacity: 0.5 },
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the ripple on Android and pressed state on iOS?
- How would you adapt the component for dark mode and dynamic icon sizing?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of bridging fundamentals and graceful degradation in RN apps.

## Key Concepts

- Native Modules bridging (Android/iOS)
- Platform-specific APIs and permissions
- Safe fallback to JS-only APIs (Vibration)
- Basic testing strategy across platforms

## Code Example

```javascript
import { NativeModules, Vibration, Platform } from 'react-native';
const { VibrateModule } = NativeModules;
export const vibrate = (ms) => {
  if (VibrateModule?.vibrate) VibrateModule.vibrate(ms);
  else if (Platform.OS !== 'web' && Vibration?.vibrate) Vibration.vibrate(ms);
};
```

